          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "A raised blood pressure reading in someone who has not been given a diagnosis of hypertension is classified in Chapter 18, the chapter for symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (codes R00 to R99). It is not coded as hypertension (I10). The source is the \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18\" summary, which lists \"an elevated blood-pressure reading in someone with no diagnosis of hypertension\" among the circulatory and respiratory codes at R00 to R09. The \"Coding hypertension: I10\" summary says the same thing: a single raised reading, or a series of readings, without a hypertension diagnosis is a finding rather than a disease. It also says the diagnosis recorded in the chart, not the number itself, decides which code applies.\n\nThe retrieved material is about how this reading is classified in medical records. It does not say what a particular blood pressure number means for any person, or what should be done about it. Those questions are best taken to a clinician who can look at the full picture.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
